# `.must()`: extract or abort (§4.6), and the abort carries the failure.
#
# The whole point of this step is the second half. `abort must` was lowered with an
# EMPTY argument list, so the only message available was "a `.must()` failed" — which
# is the one thing the reader already knows. The failure now travels with the abort, so
# the panic names the `code` and `msg` the author wrote.
#
# The read happens *inside* the abort block, so nothing counted crosses a block edge and
# `asserts.rs`'s synthetic-slot repair is not needed here — the shape panel 021 had to
# fix for `assert` does not recur for `must`.
#
# The `4` before the abort proves two things at once: `.must()` on an ok extracts, and
# `hero_panic_must` flushes stdout before writing to stderr. Without the flush the
# ordering of the two streams is undefined and this golden would be flaky.
#
# `# UNVERIFIED — pending debrief` (adversarial case 1 of 5 for M6).

function half(n: i64) -> i64?
    if n % 2 == 1
        return fail(code: "odd", msg: "not divisible by two")
    return ok(n / 2)

function main()
    print(half(8).must())
    print(half(7).must())
